{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '242,310p' collab/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan-r5.gpt-6-astra.findings.md; sed -n '485,499p' collab/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan-r5.gpt-6-astra.findings.md; git ls-files docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; ls collab/1792329904305*\"","aggregated_output":"### 4.1.1 Import ABI `yin.register.host.v1` (proposed, not existing source)\n\nThe following names are a **new implementation ABI**, not repository functions or a guest r2 schema change. All runtimes supply identical imports. The shared runtime module imports `memory` (one wasm32, non-shared memory), `functions` (one original function table), and **`exchange(control_ptr: i32) -> i32`** from module name `yin.register.host.v1`. Generated component modules import the same memory/table and the service entry as needed, plus declared immutable i32 base-offset words. No import/export passes a JS value, externref, Rust pointer, string object, multi-value result or i64 through a host-call signature. i64 pcs/numbers are bytes in memory; core i64 computation inside wasm is allowed.\n\n**Offsets and ownership.** Reserve bytes 0–255 for ABI control, initially zeroed; control_ptr is 0 for v1. At offsets +0/+4/+8/+12 store little-endian u32 magic `0x31524e59`, major 1, minor 0, header length 64. Offsets +16/+20 hold request pointer/length; +24/+28 response pointer/capacity; +32 response written length; +36 required response length; +40/+44 exchange sequence low/high; +48 transport status; +52 flags (zero in v1); +56/+60 reserved zero. Initial request region is [256, 32768), response region [32768, 65536); task allocation starts at 65536. For larger messages, allocate checked disjoint buffers in the same memory and update the descriptor; these initial sizes are not semantic limits. Neither request nor response may overlap control or machine records. W0 records maximum message/depth/arena budgets and exact qualified resource-limit results.\n\nThe guest writes the complete request/header, then invokes exchange. The host reads/copies and validates it, performs at most one transition, writes response bytes and result lengths/status, and returns the same status as i32. The host cannot grow memory or re-enter guest exports inside exchange; guest allocation/growth occurs between calls and all memory views must be refreshed afterwards. The host must bounds-check unsigned pointer+length sums without overflow. It may write only response/control output fields, not mutate the register shadow behind the guest’s back. The guest imports response state only after checking ABI/task/sequence and decoding completely.\n\n**Transport status values:** 0 COMPLETE (response available), 1 NEED_SPACE (completed response retained, required length at +36), 2 BAD_MESSAGE (malformed bytes/descriptor), 3 ABI_MISMATCH, 4 RESOURCE_LIMIT. VM execution status is a field of a successful decoded response: TRANSFER/HOST_SERVICE/YIELDED/BLOCKED/PARKED/HALTED/ERROR as already specified; guest blocking is never NEED_SPACE. Qualified semantic refusals/errors are ERROR responses containing plain diagnostic data, never host exceptions crossing the import. The CLJS public adapter raises its normal host exception only after decoding that result; native public APIs return/raise the equivalent diagnostic. Unexpected wasm traps remain implementation faults.\n\n**Exactly-once response delivery:** task+exchange sequence identifies one request; the host retains its response until the guest acknowledges it in the next request. On NEED_SPACE, resize and issue a transport `fetch-response` for that same sequence; do not replay the semantic action. Repeat fetch returns the retained bytes. A repeated sequence with different request content is BAD_MESSAGE. An effect is never repeated merely because its encoded response did not fit. A future retry after process failure is not implied by this in-memory guarantee; external effect correlation continues to use the existing stream protocol.\n\n**Plain-data encoding:** the proposed private codec has one framing rule: `u8 tag | u32 payload-byte-length (LE) | payload`. The length excludes the five-byte header. There are **no inner element counts or text code-unit counts**. Parse exactly to the enclosing byte boundary; truncated nodes, excess trailing bytes, arithmetic overflow, malformed arity and duplicate/colliding map keys or set elements are BAD_MESSAGE. A top-level message is exactly one node. The following table fixes v1 payloads; implementations must not infer a format from their serializer defaults.\n\n| Tag | Payload (no inner count) |\n|---|---|\n| 0 / 1 / 2 | Nil / false / true: length zero. |\n| 3 | Exact integer: sign byte 0/1, then minimal big-endian unsigned magnitude bytes. Zero is sign 0 with no magnitude; negative zero and leading-zero magnitudes are noncanonical. |\n| 4 | Float64: length exactly 8, IEEE-754 little-endian, with every NaN encoded as `00 00 00 00 00 00 f8 7f` (numeric bit pattern `0x7ff8000000000000`). |\n| 5 | String: only UTF-16LE code-unit bytes, even byte length; empty string has length zero. No prefix count or implicit terminator; lone surrogates round-trip in this private domain. |\n| 6 / 7 | Keyword / symbol: exactly two complete child nodes, namespace (nil or tag-5 string) and name (tag-5 string). Identifier validity follows the receiving value domain. |\n| 8 / 9 | Vector / list: zero or more complete child nodes, in original sequence order, ending exactly at outer byte boundary. |\n| 10 | Map: zero or more complete alternating key/value child nodes; odd child count is invalid. Canonical underlying-value order below is required. |\n| 11 | Set: zero or more complete child nodes, in canonical underlying-value order below. |\n| 12 | Metadata pair: exactly two child nodes, value then metadata. This private order does not replace Jing’s distinct `[meta value]` wire convention. |\n| 13 | Checked task-local arena reference: length 8, u32 id then u32 generation, both LE. Never itself a value-order key. |\n\n**Ordering independent of interning:** define expanded canonical ABI bytes of a plain value by recursively resolving every checked arena reference and encoding its value without tag 13. Map keys and set elements sort by unsigned lexicographic comparison of these complete expanded bytes (shorter prefix first); this comparator is a private ABI rule, not Jing’s ordering rule. Compute the order **before** choosing reference compression, including references nested inside compound keys. The actual emitted key/element may use a reference once sorted; decoder resolves definitions and verifies the same order. Reject duplicate canonical encodings and equality-collapsing members before constructing a host map/set. A missing/foreign reference is BAD_MESSAGE, never a numeric fallback sort key. Thus changing arena numbers, preinterning or inline-versus-reference representation cannot change entry order.\n\nFor a trusted non-plain value used as a key/element, obtain a pure, non-minting structural sort descriptor from the trusted value arena: kind plus owner/payload for Closure/Continuation, registered composition capability identity/profile for a primitive, kind/payload for a typed effect. Canonicalize the descriptor’s expanded value bytes under a distinct kind prefix, so a literal lookalike map cannot collide with a trusted type. It must contain no pointer, arena id or iteration-dependent id. Descriptor generation must neither lift resources nor allocate cursor/stream ids. W0 fixes descriptor tags alongside fixtures; a composition exposing an unnamed host callable must supply a stable capability identity for this private ordering (it remains non-portable on guest streams). Runtime-opaque values without such a descriptor cannot be advertised as supported ABI keys; this is an explicit capability admission failure, not unstable ordering or silent data loss. Trusted-value order descriptors never authorize minting the value from ordinary guest data.\n\n**Float classification and round trips:** classify a CLJS Float64 carrier before testing exact-integer?, and use `yin.vm.integer.host/exact-integer?` semantics for plain numeric inputs: plain JS safe integers are integers, **-0.0 is not**. Decode every tag-4 node into `dao.jing.cbor/float64` or `float64-from-bits` on CLJS so 1.0 and +0.0 remain floating content; Rust retains a distinct Float64 variant. Preserve all non-NaN bits, especially negative zero and infinities. Encode every NaN payload/sign as the one quiet NaN above; reject a noncanonical NaN wire node as BAD_MESSAGE. This matches the existing canonical NaN conventions in the two repository codecs, without treating ABI bytes as their hash inputs. A plain JS Number 1 without a carrier cannot recover an earlier float distinction; fixtures must construct the carrier explicitly. Exact integers beyond the safe range remain tag 3 with exact magnitude. W0 tests value→wire→value→wire identity, including integer 1 versus Float64 1.0, ±0.0, infinities and multiple NaN payloads.\n\n\nMessages are maps with ABI version, task id, sequence, acknowledgement, operation, input state revision, execution snapshot, argument values/references and newly defined arena values; responses carry sequence, output revision, execution status, result, post-service state and newly defined values or diagnostic. Every execution snapshot includes pc/registers/frames/K/store-of/value/halted/blocked, plus layout generation. Host-owned fields and immutable image/value data may be referenced by previously acknowledged definitions; missing or foreign definitions refuse. Mutation is represented by a new state revision, not mutation of a saved snapshot. Unknown extension fields in named state are preserved. W0 emits golden request/response bytes and locks required-field/type checks for each operation; both sides use those fixtures, not serializer defaults.\n\nArena-reference tag 13 is an internal task-scoped reference, not a portable value or a way for guest data to mint authority. Trusted capabilities (primitive, Closure, Continuation, typed effect, stream service) are installed by the composition or checked kernel constructors and recorded in the trusted arena. Generic literal decoding cannot create them. Plain-data descriptor maps in the service protocol may name a trusted constructor operation, but guest literal maps and FFI data never enter that operation channel. The native value service implements the same owner/type/arity and effect-authorization checks as the reference. External continuation export resolves arena refs to the payload values in §3 or refuses; it never exposes tag 13 as a guest transport representation.\n\n**Required operations:** handshake/capability-profile negotiation; value definition/lookup and trusted closure/continuation minting; free-name/store/gensym services; primitive application with typed-effect authorization and the exact §2.4 completion; stream/effect/FFI transitions; scheduler-round and ready/parked restore; collection/pinning; admitted module attachment/spawn and closure/resource lift/lower. Each operation specifies its reference CLJC transition and read/write field set in W0. Requests carry the materialized state before any service that can collect. Pure body control, register moves and guest closure calls stay compiled; the service is not allowed to “run the remaining program” to manufacture the expected trace.\n\n**Single engine port rule:** the native service library **is** the `yin-value`, `yin-scalar`, `yin-image`, `yin-kernel`, `yin-prims`, `yin-engine` and `yin-wire` workspace designed at `docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1134–1158`, extended with a handle/ABI facade and thin Wasmtime/Wasmer wrappers. It is never a parallel port of register execution, scheduler, validator, primitive semantics, custody, module stores, collection or FFI two-step. The facade decodes validated messages, resolves checked handles, invokes the shared transition functions and serializes their committed outcomes. It owns only transport/arena/runtime mechanics. The Node adapter decodes into existing CLJS records and calls existing CLJC helpers. Native execution cannot be replay, a JS subprocess or success-only stubs; missing required semantics fails the slice.\n\nThe “one transition implementation, two dispatchers” rule (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:991–995, 1308–1311`) applies across these execution strategies: CLJS kernel and CLJS wasm adapter reach the same CLJC transition; Rust interpreter, native wasm facade and later LLVM helpers reach the same Rust transition. Across CLJC and Rust there is necessarily a language port, governed by one specification and one conformance kit—not literally one callable binary. Within either host, a second service-specific transition implementation is a defect. Generated constants/moves/branches specialize the admitted instruction operands; generated service sites never reproduce call/return/restore/effect logic in a wrapper. Extract reusable transition helpers in the owning crate if necessary, rather than dispatching a second copy of the engine.\n\nPreserve check-then-commit atomicity and fault containment (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:639–713`): refused transitions leave guest state unchanged; a panic is a kernel defect, caught before crossing the runtime boundary, poisons only its machine, and cannot become a success payload. ABI malformed-message refusals precede semantic work; retained responses preserve once-only effect completion. Safe facade code uses checked slices and Value operations. Do not expand the unsafe surface beyond the Rust design’s explicit boundary crates (lines 1154–1158); any unavoidable runtime binding belongs to a reviewed boundary, never the kernel/engine. Imported service calls are internal execution plumbing; guest external effects still use stream emissions, not arbitrary synchronous host callbacks.\n\nThe trade-off is explicit: every host-service invocation on every embedder serializes/deserializes linear-memory messages. Acknowledged immutable definitions reduce copying but do not bypass the ABI. W6 measures this cost and may move value/scheduler services into a self-contained wasm interpreter/runtime, leaving the ABI only at stream/FFI boundaries. That is an optimization axis, not a portability prerequisite or a claim of present speed.\n\n### 4.1.2 Deterministic service traversal (shared prerequisite)\n\nSource verification found real unsorted map/set walks that can mint ids or choose diagnostics. Rust must not reproduce them with its default map order, and canonicalizing only the final ABI bytes is too late. Introduce an explicit **shared deterministic traversal policy in the CLJC helpers and the native service**, then use that revised reference for wasm parity. This is a planned semantic stabilization of previously host-order-dependent behavior, not an already satisfied property of current source and not a wasm-only patch. No source edit is performed by this report.\n\nFor currently unordered traversal sites use a pure order key: for ordinary machine values, the fully expanded canonical ABI value bytes defined above; for trusted values, their non-minting structural descriptors. Sort maps by key and sets by element, using unsigned lexicographic comparison, never arena numbering, hash-table iteration or the output of a resource-lifting encoder. Build keys for a collection before invoking any id-minting traversal of that collection. Retain vector/list order, ordered exports, wait/ready queue order and first-occurrence deduplication. Retain **existing explicit source orderings** such as sort-by str on cursor ids/module-store addresses and sort-by key on attached image identities, with cross-host fixtures for those comparators; do not replace them with ABI order under the guise of fixing unordered maps.\n\n| Site (existing symbols in engine.cljc unless qualified) | Required shared traversal change / preserved order |\n|---|---|\n| `encoder`, `encode-store` | Sort raw literal-map/store keys before visiting key then value; sort sets before recursively encoding. Never sort the already encoded output: `cell-for!` has already minted `:yin.k/c-N` by then. Keep vector/list and explicit export sequence order. |\n| `markers` | Maintain pre-order tree walk; for maps visit values in sorted-key order (preserve the source’s values-only walk), for sets sort elements, for vectors/lists preserve sequence order. No incidental host map order may determine first stream discovery. |\n| `lower-resources` | Preserve sorted used-cursor ids and the direct-stream-marker pass followed by cursor-derived streams; consume canonically traversed marker sequences, deduplicate by stream identity at first encounter, then mint gensym ids. Do not reorder queues or change aliasing of repeated cursor cells. |\n| `receive-module` | Keep its explicit sorted image attach order. Enumerate slice values and fresh-store values in sorted-key order before lower-resources; use the same order for bindings/store decoding and deterministic error selection. Preserve slice-before-fresh-store phase order and first-link-wins stores. |\n| `module/module-entries`, `module/list-modules` (module.cljc) | Walk registry child maps in sorted-key order and return top-level module names in that order. Missing-module `:available` vectors and registry consumers no longer inherit host map iteration. |\n| `resume-from-run-queue` | Keep FIFO entry selection and terminal-check ordering; sort resource-update keys before selecting the first non-keyword diagnostic. Preserve updates and merge semantics. |\n| `lift-slice`, `decode-store` and adjacent consumers | Preserve ordered export input; if a collection is a set, sort before visitation. Audit downstream unordered traversal for id minting, first-error choice or observable vectors, and route those paths through this policy. |\n\nThe helpers named above exist; the ordering policy/helper itself is proposed implementation work. It must land with shared CLJC/native regression tests before the corresponding W3/W4 acceptance gate. This deliberately can change old host-specific `c-N` assignments, stream ids/seals, slice-entry vectors or diagnostic order; record those deltas as the named prerequisite, never normalize them out. A fixed CLJS-only historical map order is not a cross-runtime contract. Existing canonical R/Jing hash algorithms and frozen byte corpora remain unchanged.\n\nW0 inventories unordered observation sites and freezes order-key fixtures. W3 implements resource-update diagnostic order and validates counters/seals; W4 completes module lift/lower ordering and tests equal maps built with different insertion histories, small and large maps spanning CLJS map representations, nested maps/sets, repeated stream/cursor refs, multi-store modules and multiple invalid update keys. Compare exact minted ids, seals, `:yin.k/entries`, `:cells`, `:available`, counters and resource maps. A parity failure here blocks the slice; it cannot be hidden as implementation-private arena variation.\n\n### 4.2 Stream and park transitions\n## Revision log and reference check\n\n- **r1:** established the 23-opcode r2 lowering, i32 ValueRef arena, resumable body functions, sparse guest continuations, shared scheduler semantics and W0–W6.\n- **r2:** incorporated the seven source-verified round-1 findings. The complete response table and its correct citations above are retained unchanged. The orchestrator reports that r2 was approved; that approval is not used as evidence for source behavior.\n- **r3:** incorporates the binding portability requirement. Existing semantic transition rules remain the oracle; new ABI names, wire tags, service operations, native library and harness tasks in this document are explicitly proposed implementation artifacts, not claims of existing repository functions.\n\n- **r4:** verified all four round-3 findings and added separate native repository-codec/hash conformance, unambiguous deterministic ABI value encoding, an explicit shared traversal stabilization prerequisite, and earlier transport/Wasmer/module-slice gates. Prior review responses and the Round 3 changes section are retained as historical records; r4’s additions supersede earlier omissions about ordering and tests.\n\n- **r5:** reconciled the Rust-kernel revision-2 authority, single Rust service port, fixed native Value facade, shared K0/W0 kit and LLVM sibling/cache law; explicitly superseded only the wasm-artifact deferral and documented the scope/identity/index/step/Level-2 scheduling reconciliations. Prior response sections remain historical evidence.\n\nReference convention: `register.cljc` means `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc`; `engine`, `ffi`, `module`, `values`, and `debruijn_register_effects` filenames are under `src/cljc/yin/vm/`; `vm` means `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`; `apply2` is the alias for `dao.stream.apply` at `src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc`. Unqualified register helpers such as return-transition, write-back, payload-of, park-entry-fns, attach-image and spawn-module are in register.cljc; spawn-module/gc-roots are protocol implementations, not invented defns. `module/IModuleKernel` and its gc-roots method are declared in module.cljc. IVM step/run are methods of `yin.vm/IVM`, not a namespace named IVM. `effects/*` names denote debruijn_register_effects.cljc. The round-1 response table’s abbreviated `:line` entries continue the immediately preceding source file, not a new path. `register-image-defect`, `body-liveness`, `live-slot-index`, `register-hash` and `opcode-table` belong to `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc` (alias rcode); the `register-image` derivation belongs to `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc`. `halted-with-empty-queue?`, `scheduler-round`, `check-wait-set`, `handle-close` and `collect` are engine.cljc functions. The quoted restore/call helper names are existing source semantics, whereas exchange/fetch-response, ABI statuses and all native implementation entry names are proposed protocol vocabulary.\n\nFor this revision, the existing qualified symbols used in the plan were checked in those source files: the engine resolution/store/operator/effect/scheduler/pinning/encoder helpers; FFI call-pair/request/response/result/bridge helpers; module effect and kernel protocol symbols; value constructors/payload accessor; machine-data?/gate-mode; continuation-payload/continuation-defect/wait-entry-defect; and apply2/request. The opcode table still has **23** entries and the value representation remains **i32 handles**, not NaN boxing. No new runtime execution result is claimed by this documentation check.\n\n## Round 3 changes\ncollab/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan-r2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\ncollab/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan-r3.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\ncollab/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan-r4.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\ncollab/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan-r5.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\ncollab/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\ncollab/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan.gpt-6-astra.prompt.md\ncollab/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
